  <!-- ── 1 · WHAT g/m² ACTUALLY MEASURES ───────────────────── -->
  <section class="fab wrap" id="measures">
    <div class="sec__head">
      <h2 class="sec__h">What grams per square metre actually measures</h2>
      <span class="mono">Mass ÷ area</span>
    </div>

    <div class="fab__lead">
      <div>
        <p class="fab__p"><strong>It is the weight of one square metre of the fabric.</strong>
          Nothing more. A 220 g/m² knit is one where a square metre of it, cut flat and
          weighed, comes to 220 grams. Mills measure it with a die cutter that punches a
          disc of known area and a balance that reads to a hundredth of a gram. The
          published test methods are ISO 3801 and ASTM D3776. It is the least ambiguous
          figure on a specification sheet, which is a fair part of why so few brands
          print it.</p>

        <p class="fab__p"><strong>Weight is a proxy for knit density.</strong>
          For a given fibre and yarn type, more grams in a square metre means more fibre
          packed into that square metre — more material sitting between a light source
          and whatever is behind the fabric. Density is what light has to get through.
          That is the whole mechanism. It is not a feeling, and it does not depend on
          anyone's judgement.</p>

        <p class="fab__p"><strong>Stretch is why the resting figure matters.</strong>
          When a knit is stretched, its area grows and its mass does not. Pull a fabric
          to 1.3 times its resting area and every square metre of it now holds 1.3 times
          less material: at that instant a 220 g/m² knit is behaving optically like a
          169 g/m² one, and a 180 g/m² knit like a 138 g/m² one. That is arithmetic, not
          a test result — the 1.3× figure is an illustration, not a measurement of any
          particular garment on any particular body. The point survives whatever number
          you substitute. The weight on a spec sheet is the weight at rest, and the
          useful question is how much headroom it leaves once the garment is stretched
          over the glute and the wearer moves.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <p class="fab__p"><strong>This is why "compression" and "buttery" answer nothing.</strong>
          Neither word carries a unit, a test method or a number. Hand-feel is real and
          people care about it, but it is not measurable and it has no relationship to
          whether a fabric goes translucent when it is stretched. Density does. A brand
          that describes a fabric only in adjectives has told you nothing you can verify
          against another brand.</p>

        <p class="fab__p"><strong>Where weight stops being enough.</strong>
          Two fabrics at the same g/m² can differ in opacity, and it is worth saying so
          plainly. Yarn construction matters — many fine filaments cover better than
          fewer coarse ones at the same mass. So do knit gauge and knit structure.
          Colour matters a great deal: a pale or heathered knit shows through at a weight
          where the identical fabric in black does not. Finishing — brushing, calendering,
          coating — moves it again.</p>

        <p class="fab__p">
          Fabric weight is the single most useful number a brand can publish about a
          stretch knit, and it is not the only variable. Anyone who tells you it settles
          the question on its own is selling something.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── 2 · WEIGHT BANDS ──────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="fab fab--alt" id="bands">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec__head">
        <h2 class="sec__h">A reference table of weight bands</h2>
        <span class="mono">Industry convention · stretch knits</span>
      </div>

      <div class="fab__tw">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Band</th>
              <th scope="col">g/m²</th>
              <th scope="col">Typical use</th>
              <th scope="col">Behaviour under stretch</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="fab__band">Sheer</th>
              <td class="fab__num">120–150</td>
              <td>Mesh panels, vents, jersey liners, sheer overlays</td>
              <td class="fab__beh">Translucent at rest in most colours, and effectively
                see-through once stretched. Used deliberately for ventilation, not as a
                main body fabric.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="fab__band">Light</th>
              <td class="fab__num">150–180</td>
              <td>T-shirts, loose layers, some lightweight yoga leggings</td>
              <td class="fab__beh">Covers at rest. Thins visibly as soon as it is
                stretched. Fine for anything worn loose, marginal for anything worn
                tight.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="fab__band">Standard</th>
              <td class="fab__num">180–200</td>
              <td>The most common legging and short weight in this price bracket</td>
              <td class="fab__beh">Opaque standing still. Thins under deep stretch — the
                seated position and the bottom of a squat are where it shows. Pale and
                heathered colours show it first.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="fab__band">Dense</th>
              <td class="fab__num">200–220</td>
              <td>Leggings specified for training rather than lounging</td>
              <td class="fab__beh">Generally holds opacity through the same stretch that
                thins a standard knit. Firmer hand, slower to dry, harder to pull on.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="fab__band">Heavy</th>
              <td class="fab__num">220–260</td>
              <td>Heavyweight leggings, "sculpt" and "contour" knits, some bike shorts</td>
              <td class="fab__beh">Holds opacity with headroom. Noticeably stiff, warmer,
                and slower to dry. Above roughly 260 the trade-off is usually not worth
                it in a legging.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="fab__band">Fleece</th>
              <td class="fab__num">260–320</td>
              <td>Sweatshirts, joggers, fleece-backed sets</td>
              <td class="fab__beh">Little stretch by comparison, so opacity is rarely the
                constraint. Weight here buys warmth and durability, not coverage.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="disc fab__disc">
        <p><strong>These bands are convention, not measurement</strong>
          No standards body defines them. They are the rough lines mills, buyers and
          technical designers draw when they talk about weight in this category, and
          different suppliers draw them in different places — one mill's "mid-weight" is
          another mill's "light". They describe stretch knits used in activewear only: a
          200 g/m² woven shirting behaves nothing like a 200 g/m² knit. <em>Nothing in
          this table is a measurement of a HIVOLT garment.</em> For any HIVOLT style, the
          figure that applies is the one printed on its own product page, taken from the
          mill's specification sheet.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── 3 · CHECK IT YOURSELF ─────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="fab wrap" id="measure-it">
    <div class="sec__head">
      <h2 class="sec__h">How to check any brand's claim yourself</h2>
      <span class="mono">Ours included</span>
    </div>

    <p class="fab__p fab__p--lead">
      A scale, a tape measure and one division. The method is approximate — call it
      ±5% — which is not good enough to argue about five grams and is easily good
      enough to catch a claim that is wrong by thirty.
    </p>

    <ol class="fab__steps">
      <li><strong>Weigh the garment.</strong> A digital kitchen scale that resolves 1 g
        is enough. Weigh it dry, clean and on its own. If a card hangtag is still
        attached, weigh the tag separately and subtract it.</li>
      <li><strong>Lay it flat and unstretched</strong> on a hard floor. Smooth the
        wrinkles out with a flat hand. Do not pull it.</li>
      <li><strong>Measure the outline in centimetres</strong> and break it into
        rectangles. For a legging: one rectangle from the top of the waistband down to
        the crotch seam (flat width × height), plus one for each leg (the average of the
        flat width at the top and at the hem, × the inseam).</li>
      <li><strong>Add the rectangles up.</strong> That total is the flat outline area in
        square centimetres.</li>
      <li><strong>Double it.</strong> A garment laid flat is two layers of fabric — front
        panel and back panel — everywhere inside that outline.</li>
      <li><strong>Divide by 10,000</strong> to convert square centimetres to square
        metres.</li>
      <li><strong>Divide the weight in grams by the area in square metres.</strong> The
        answer is grams per square metre.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3 class="fab__sub">Worked through once, with real numbers</h3>
    <div class="sheet fab__sheet">
      <div class="sheet__row"><span class="sheet__k">Scale reading, size M legging</span><span class="sheet__v">123 g</span></div>
      <div class="sheet__row"><span class="sheet__k">Waistband to crotch, 36 × 29 cm</span><span class="sheet__v">1,044 cm²</span></div>
      <div class="sheet__row"><span class="sheet__k">One leg, 13.5 cm avg × 78 cm inseam</span><span class="sheet__v">1,053 cm²</span></div>
      <div class="sheet__row"><span class="sheet__k">Flat outline, 1,044 + (2 × 1,053)</span><span class="sheet__v">3,150 cm²</span></div>
      <div class="sheet__row"><span class="sheet__k">Two layers, 3,150 × 2</span><span class="sheet__v">6,300 cm²</span></div>
      <div class="sheet__row"><span class="sheet__k">As square metres, 6,300 ÷ 10,000</span><span class="sheet__v">0.63 m²</span></div>
      <div class="sheet__row"><span class="sheet__k">Result, 123 ÷ 0.63</span><span class="sheet__v"><em>195 g/m²</em></span></div>
      <p class="sheet__src">
        195 lands in the 180–200 band. If that garment's label said 220, the label is
        wrong by far more than this method's error. If the label said 200, you have
        learned nothing — the gap is inside the noise.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="fab__lead fab__lead--tight">
      <div>
        <h3 class="fab__sub">What this method will not tell you</h3>
        <ul class="fab__list">
          <li>Waistbands are two or more layers and are often a different, heavier knit
            with elastic inside them. They pull the answer up.</li>
          <li>Seam allowances, the gusset and the hems add fabric that is not inside the
            flat outline. Also up.</li>
          <li>A panelled garment mixes fabrics, so what you get is a weighted average
            across all of them, not the weight of the main body knit.</li>
          <li>Those errors mostly push the same way, so treat your result as an upper
            bound on the main fabric rather than a reading of it.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="fab__sub">The exact method, if you can spare the fabric</h3>
        <p class="fab__p">Cut a square exactly 10 cm × 10 cm. That is 0.01 m². Weigh it
          and multiply by 100. A 10 cm square weighing 2.20 g is a 220 g/m² fabric; one
          weighing 1.85 g is 185 g/m².</p>
        <p class="fab__p">This is what a mill does, with a circular die cutter and a lab
          balance instead of scissors and a kitchen scale. It destroys the garment, so do
          it on a scrap, on an offcut, or on something you have already written off. It
          is the only version of this that is exact.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      Our own published figures, so the method above has something to be pointed at.
      Values come from the spec.gsm metafield only. A product without one is omitted
      rather than guessed at.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- capture gsm_rows -%}
      {%- for p in collections.all.products limit: 24 -%}
        {%- assign g = p.metafields.spec.gsm -%}
        {%- if g != blank -%}
          <a class="sheet__row" href="{{ p.url }}">
            <span class="sheet__k">{{ p.title }}</span>
            <span class="sheet__v"><em>{{ g }} g/m²</em></span>
          </a>
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    {%- endcapture -%}

    {%- if gsm_rows != blank -%}
      <h3 class="fab__sub">Our published figures, so you have something to check</h3>
      <div class="sheet fab__sheet">
        {{ gsm_rows }}
        <p class="sheet__src">
          Every figure above is the fabric weight from the mill's specification sheet for
          that style, copied unedited, and is the same number shown on the product page.
          Where a style has no published figure it is not listed here rather than
          estimated. Measure one and tell us if we are wrong —
          {{ shop.email | default: 'support@hivolt-usa.com' }}.
        </p>
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}
  </section>

  <!-- ── 4 · FIBRE GLOSSARY ────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="fab wrap" id="fibres">
    <div class="sec__head">
      <h2 class="sec__h">Fibre glossary</h2>
      <span class="mono">What each one contributes</span>
    </div>

    <div class="trust__grid fab__fib">
      <div class="trust__c">
        <div class="trust__k">Polyamide</div>
        <p class="trust__v">Nylon</p>
        <p class="trust__d">The softer and stronger of the two common synthetics. At a
          given weight it takes more abrasion before the surface goes fuzzy, and it has a
          smoother, more fluid drape than polyester.</p>
        <ul class="fab__attr">
          <li><b>Hand</b><span>Smooth, soft, fluid. The reason a nylon blend feels
            different from a polyester one at identical weight.</span></li>
          <li><b>Moisture</b><span>Absorbs a few per cent of its own weight in water, so
            it dries more slowly and feels heavier when soaked.</span></li>
          <li><b>Durability</b><span>Higher tensile and abrasion resistance than
            polyester; less resistant to prolonged sunlight.</span></li>
          <li><b>Cost</b><span>More expensive per kilo, which is why it appears more
            often above a certain price point.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="trust__c">
        <div class="trust__k">Polyester</div>
        <p class="trust__v">Polyester</p>
        <p class="trust__d">Dimensionally stable, resistant to sunlight and most
          chemicals, and it holds dyed colour well. The default fibre for activewear at
          volume, on cost as much as on performance.</p>
        <ul class="fab__attr">
          <li><b>Hand</b><span>Drier and crisper than nylon at the same
            construction.</span></li>
          <li><b>Moisture</b><span>Absorbs almost nothing — well under 1% of its weight.
            Water travels along the outside of the fibre rather than into it, which is
            what the word "wicking" describes, and it dries fast.</span></li>
          <li><b>Durability</b><span>Very good UV and chemical resistance; holds shape
            and printed colour over time.</span></li>
          <li><b>Trade-off</b><span>The same oil-friendly surface holds body oils, which
            is why polyester activewear can keep odour through a cold wash.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="trust__c">
        <div class="trust__k">Elastomeric</div>
        <p class="trust__v">Spandex / elastane</p>
        <p class="trust__d">One fibre, three names: spandex in the United States,
          elastane in Europe, and Lycra as a trade name for a particular maker's version
          of it. It is never the whole fabric — it is blended in.</p>
        <ul class="fab__attr">
          <li><b>Contributes</b><span>Recovery. It stretches several times its own length
            and pulls back, which is what returns a fabric to shape instead of letting it
            bag at the knee and seat.</span></li>
          <li><b>Proportion</b><span>Commonly 5–30% by weight of the finished fabric. The
            percentage is by weight, not by how much stretch you feel — knit structure
            decides that as much as the blend does.</span></li>
          <li><b>Limits</b><span>Degraded by chlorine, high heat and oils. That is the
            reason care labels on these fabrics say cold wash and no tumble dry.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="sheet__src fab__note">
      Composition percentages are by weight of the finished fabric, and they tell you
      nothing about g/m² on their own. A 75/25 nylon-spandex blend exists at 160 g/m² and
      at 240 g/m², and those are not the same garment. Composition and weight are two
      independent numbers, and a specification sheet that gives you one without the other
      has given you half of it.
    </p>
  </section>

