  <!-- ── 6 · QUESTIONS (mirrored in FAQPage JSON-LD below) ──── -->
  <section class="faq" id="questions">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="faq__h">Questions about fabric weight</h2>
      <p class="mono fab__faqnote">Answered from the page above</p>

      <div class="faq__list">
        <details>
          <summary>What does g/m² mean on a fabric label?</summary>
          <p class="faq__a">Grams per square metre is the weight of one square metre of
            that fabric. A 220 g/m² knit is one where a square metre of it, cut flat and
            weighed, comes to 220 grams. It is measured to ISO 3801 or ASTM D3776 by
            cutting a disc of known area and weighing it on a laboratory balance. For a
            given fibre and yarn it is a direct proxy for knit density.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Why does a legging go translucent when it is stretched?</summary>
          <p class="faq__a">Because stretching increases the fabric's area while its mass
            stays the same, so there is less material in every square metre of it. A
            fabric pulled to 1.3 times its resting area behaves optically like one that
            is 1.3 times lighter — a 220 g/m² knit like a 169 g/m² one. The weight quoted
            on a specification sheet is the weight at rest, so it tells you how much
            headroom is left once the garment is stretched over the body.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What fabric weight stays opaque?</summary>
          <p class="faq__a">By industry convention, stretch knits at 200–220 g/m² and
            above generally hold opacity through deep stretch, and 180–200 g/m² is the
            common legging weight that thins under it. No standards body defines those
            bands, different mills draw them in different places, and colour, yarn
            construction and knit gauge all move the line — a pale or heathered knit
            shows through at a weight where the same fabric in black does not.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How do I measure the fabric weight of a garment at home?</summary>
          <p class="faq__a">Weigh the garment in grams on a kitchen scale. Lay it flat
            and unstretched, measure its outline in centimetres and break that into
            rectangles. Double the total, because a garment laid flat is two layers of
            fabric. Divide by 10,000 to get square metres, then divide the weight by the
            area. Worked example: 123 g over a 6,300 cm² two-layer area, which is
            0.63 m², gives 195 g/m². Expect about ±5%, because waistbands, seam
            allowances and gussets add fabric that is not in the outline. The exact
            method is to cut a 10 cm × 10 cm square, weigh it and multiply by 100.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What is the difference between nylon and polyester in activewear?</summary>
          <p class="faq__a">Nylon is softer and more abrasion-resistant, drapes more
            fluidly, absorbs a few per cent of its weight in water so dries more slowly,
            and costs more. Polyester absorbs almost nothing, so it dries faster and
            wicks along the fibre surface, resists sunlight and chemicals better, holds
            colour well, and is cheaper — but its oil-friendly surface tends to hold
            odour. Spandex, also called elastane and sold under the Lycra trade name, is
            blended into either at roughly 5–30% by weight and supplies recovery: the
            return to shape that stops a fabric bagging out.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

