      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Weigh the garment in grams on a kitchen scale. Lay it flat and unstretched, measure its outline in centimetres and break that into rectangles. Double the total, because a garment laid flat is two layers of fabric. Divide by 10,000 to convert to square metres, then divide the weight by the area. Worked example: 123 g over a 6,300 cm² two-layer area, which is 0.63 m², gives 195 g/m². Expect roughly plus or minus 5 per cent, because waistbands, seam allowances and gussets add fabric that is not inside the flat outline. The exact method is to cut a 10 cm by 10 cm square, weigh it, and multiply by 100."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the difference between nylon and polyester in activewear?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Nylon is softer and more abrasion-resistant, drapes more fluidly, absorbs a few per cent of its weight in water so dries more slowly, and costs more per kilo. Polyester absorbs almost nothing, so it dries faster and wicks along the fibre surface, resists sunlight and chemicals better, and holds colour well, but its oil-friendly surface tends to hold odour. Spandex, also called elastane and sold under the Lycra trade name, is blended into either at roughly 5 to 30 per cent by weight and supplies recovery: the return to shape that stops a fabric bagging out."
      }
    }
  ]
